<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#zamindar">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zamindar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A zamindar was the holder of an estate, a zamindari, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, typically hereditary and with the right to collect taxes; the Persian word for landowner came into use under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. The word covered autonomous chiefs, two to three hundred under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> by one contemporary count, and intermediaries who collected revenue from the peasants but did not own the land. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s Permanent Settlement of 1793 made zamindars proprietors for a fixed annual rent, and land reforms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> abolished the system in the 1950s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zamindar.jpg" alt="Zamindar" width="215" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mehtab_Chand_of_Burdwan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
